Finite12-case contract

The exact scenario-roster.json is required by the collector; no test filter or skip fallback is available. Three Flow cases use actual CRLF/bareCR/LF file uploads containing U+2028, astral and combining Unicode, save/deploy and actual browser file downloads with exact decoded raw UTF-8 bytes. Additional Flow cases check dirty deployment refusal before API transport, pending actual Save and export validation guards, disposal while validation is held, static Link In/Out diagrams and real SVG pointer dragging. Two pending Flow draft probes explicitly bypass a disabled native textarea with programmatic DOM value+input changes; observations identify them as adversarial bypasses, not ordinary typing. Source review caught and corrected the original fill()-while-disabled deadlock before any execution.

Vault uses an owned synthetic KDBX document generated by the existing current candidate exportKdbx/group-fixture helper and imports it through the actual browser file controls. This fixture is not an independently generated original KeePass file and no new interoperability claim is made. The four Vault cases cover committed Create/Save final-list draft retention, explicit Save to the newly created ID, dirty entry/group gates and post-commit disposal scrubbing. Vault group controls remain editable during the held final-list interval, so those newer drafts use normal browser fill/input actions.

The timing holds use page.route + route.fetch to execute the real host handler and observe its actual successful response before delaying delivery. Original response status/headers/body are fulfilled unchanged; no API output is mocked. Direct API requests are fixture setup/independent persistence observations and are recorded separately from page-generated requests. Each case starts the unchanged start({port:0,dataDir}) host in its own freshly created RUNNER_TEMP directory, uses the returned random loopback port and token, and observes owned close() within5seconds. No existing listener is reused. Candidate operations affect only synthetic owned data.

The unchanged host does not serve a favicon. An actual console404 specifically for the owned /favicon.ico URL is preserved as an explicit expected auxiliary resource observation; page exceptions and application/API/other resource errors fail. This is a finite UI acceptance scope, not favicon acceptance.

Bounds, evidence and acceptance

One standard public-repository GitHub-hosted windows-2022 job, pinned Node24.16.0 and the three previously verified full action SHAs. Main pushes plus relevant PR/manual triggers avoid duplicate branch push runs.20-minute job;3-minute locked tooling install;4-minute pinned browser acquisition;8-minute Playwright global budget inside a9-minute owned process-tree deadline. One worker, zero retries, twelve explicit successful results, zero skipped/flaky/failed cases. Owned taskkill cleanup is bounded; the collector refuses if stream close was not observed. Console loss does not replace independent fsynced raw logs. No paid runner, deployed endpoint, cache action, service, local fallback or external recipient is used.

Acquisition and browser attempt each have write-exclusive initial then fsynced terminal receipts, same SHA/run/attempt/repository binding and capped independent stdout4MiB/stderr1MiB. Before the browser scenarios, all active apps/lib/public/browser harness sources and the host/catalog/collector are hashed and saved as inert snapshots; results/.local/node_modules are excluded. Capture refuses more than2000 files, an8MiB file,64MiB total or depth128, and snapshot bytes must match the before hashes. This wider normal-host source closure does not claim behavior of the eight applications outside the finite Flow/Vault cases. Post-run source hashes and clean Git state must match. Every passed case requires its owned server-close observation, actual browser/HTTP marker and no unexpected error. The collector requires all twelve exact titles, one passed zero-retry result each, twelve PNGs and three exact real download byte identities. Screenshot files are observations, not a pixel-diff baseline. Video is disabled; traces are failure-only.

Portable artifact15MiB cap, retention1day, no browser/node_modules/vendor binaries or synthetic data directories in evidence. Oversize packaging fails and uploads only a bounded explicitly incomplete diagnostic fallback when it fits. Acceptance requires a passed browser attempt with stable source, successful workflow including packaging/upload and a complete readable local backup. No browser acceptance is currently established. The existing backup unpacker needs a small future extension to recognize browser-attempt.json/browser-source-before.json/browser-source-after.json as an independent schema; existing development attempt interpretation must remain unchanged.

Commands intended only after reviewed candidate publication on the hosted runner:
node products/rea-studio/scripts/verify-cloud-browser.mjs --phase acquire --evidence <absolute owned RUNNER_TEMP child>
node products/rea-studio/scripts/verify-cloud-browser.mjs --phase verify --evidence <same child>

Independent source review of the twelve cases is recorded separately in work/cloud-recovery-20261015/hosted-flow-vault-browser-cases-independent-source-review-20261015.json. The reviewer confirmed the corrected programmatic probe labeling/logic, with actual hosted execution still pending. Collector/workflow root review and actual npm/Chromium execution remain pending.
